- All interactive elements (buttons, inputs, labels) must derive their base appearance from `global.scss` and `variables.scss`. Component-specific SCSS files must NOT redefine button backgrounds, colors, borders, font families, or input field styles that are already established globally.
- Component SCSS files should only contain styles that are **uniquely specific to that component's layout and positioning** — grid column counts, flex direction, absolute positioning offsets, aspect ratios, and similar structural concerns.
- Shared visual patterns (surface colors, text colors, border styles, border-radius, font-size scaling) must use the tokens defined in `variables.scss` (e.g. `$color-button-bg`, `$color-view-surface-fg`, `$color-view-surface-border`). Do not invent new color values inline in component files.
- Use `@include cq-responsive((...))` from `mixins.scss` for any dimension that should scale with container size (font-size, padding, gap, width, border-radius). Do not use fixed `px` or `rem` values for these properties inside view components that live within `view-content`.
- Elements that appear across multiple views (e.g. `view-title`, `button`, `input[type="text"]`) must be styled once in `global.scss`. If a component needs a size or spacing override for one of these elements, it should only set the differing property (e.g. a specific `font-size` via `cq-responsive`), not re-declare the full element style.
